====================================================================================================
6. THE ADDED-ROW ACCOUNTING for the three row-adding pairs
====================================================================================================

  A row-adding pair's whole-process instruction step gets NO VERDICT (section 1). What CAN
  be asked of it is narrower and is arithmetic: does the step EQUAL the rows the later arm
  added? The added rows' own wall is in the CSV; multiplied by the EARLIER arm's own
  whole-process instruction rate it gives an estimate of what they cost. If the estimate
  accounts for the step, the rows the two arms SHARE did not move.

  THIS IS AN ESTIMATE AND ITS ASSUMPTION IS STATED: it charges the added rows the process's
  AVERAGE instructions per second. The arm-to-arm spread of that rate is printed, and the
  residual is bounded with the extreme rates rather than the central one.

  Instruction rate across the eleven arms: 9.548 to 9.860 e9/s (spread 3.3 %)

  T8.2 (a02 -> a03) adds 4 row(s):
